course: dictionary
lesson: 13127
title: Fodder: food, material and more
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. «Fodder» is a good example. Look at the words around it to find out which meaning the writer means, then check by swapping in a simple word to see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «The farmer stored fodder in the barn so the cows would have food all winter.» The clues are farmer, barn, cows and winter. Here fodder means food for farm animals, such as hay, straw or grain.
explain: «The silly video became fodder for the comedians on the radio.» There is no animal and no food here. The clue is comedians, so fodder means material that people can easily use for jokes. Try the swap: "The silly video became material for the comedians" still works.
explain: Some words belong to set phrases. «Cannon fodder» does not mean food at all. It is a harsh phrase for soldiers treated as if their lives did not matter, so always read the whole phrase before you decide what it means.
fact: Fodder is food such as hay, straw or grain given to farm animals like cows, horses and sheep.
fact: Informally, fodder is material that is easily used for a purpose, often without much thought, as in «fodder for jokes».
fact: «Cannon fodder» means soldiers treated as if their lives do not matter, a sad phrase from history, especially the First World War.
fact: Farmers store fodder so their animals have food in winter.
fact: The context tells you which meaning of a word is right.
exercise: «The farmer stored fodder in the barn for the cold winter.» What is fodder here? | *Food such as hay for farm animals | A kind of farmhouse roof | A type of farm machine | A soft blanket for the cows
exercise: «The silly video became fodder for the comedians.» What does fodder mean here? | *Material that is easily used for jokes | A small field | Food for horses | A loud noise
exercise: «Many young soldiers were treated as cannon fodder.» What does this phrase suggest? | Soldiers who played music | Soldiers who cooked for the army | Soldiers who won every battle | *Soldiers whose lives were treated as if they did not matter
test: «The story about the mix-up was fodder for jokes all week.» What does fodder mean here? | *Material people use easily for jokes | Food for animals | A type of pasture | A fast race
test: In which sentence does fodder mean food for farm animals? | The silly rumour became fodder for gossip. | The comedian used the news as fodder for his show. | Cannon fodder was a sad phrase in the war. | *The farm stored fodder for the cows in winter.
exam: Which sentence uses fodder in the informal sense, not as animal food? | The horses ate their fodder quickly. | *The silly post became fodder for jokes online. | Cows need fodder in winter. | The farmer stored fodder in the barn.
